  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 104 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nWhile the trough and rain chances move east, this should be a nice pattern. \nHowever, once the trough gets near central Oklahoma, the long fetch\nof subtropical moisture disappear faster than kids being told to \nclean up. More scientifically, the moisture plume evident on water\nvapor imagery today...finally dries up. While we may still see \nscattered rainfall or thunderstorms Monday or Tuesday, the chances\ndecrease to 10-30% as the main trough approaches overhead. Latest\nensemble blends put the rainfall amounts in our forecast area \nbetween 0 and about 1/2 inch. Rainfall would be more welcome, most\nof us won't see much more than a few showers. \n\nLater in the week, the range of possibilities widens further. Long\nrange forecasting is hard enough, but check out these ranges for OKC:\n\nThursday morning ensemble forecast guidance \nLow temp: between 48 and 61 F\nHigh temp: between 66 and 76 F\nPoP: between 1% and 46% \nPrecip: between 0.00 and 0.89\" \n\nand that is just Thursday morning. The same wide ranges will\ncontinue into the Halloween weekend, although it is just out of \nthis official forecast period. However, there is a potential for \nanother heavy rain event just after this forecast range. \n\nBut...what is known now is there is strong (more than 60% of\nmembers) are showing increasing rain chances beginning on \nThursday, as another upper level trough approaches, along with \nanother fetch of subtropical jet. While the timing is uncertain, \nwith the evidence in the wide range of ensemble data, it does \nappear that a pattern change to a wet pattern is likely by the end\nof next weekend. However, it is too early to make final decisions\nbased on all of this, especially with the uncertainty of timing. \n\nFox",
